  "findings": {
    "strong": [
      "Channel a0dc appeared once as offered DataSeq b6e3 and never in the post-loss capture. Zero capture kernel drops and zero visible-header errors are recorded.",
      "Received b763, 128 ahead, triggered local slide b6e3 to b6e4 while the prior bitmap marked b6e3 unreceived and no peer AoA had retired it.",
      "Outgoing ACKVEC base b6e4 matches successful ledger event49745 byte-for-byte. This lower-edge advance has neither a received-prefix nor peer-AoA cause.",
      "The first offered/received Windows AoA retiring b6e3 follows that vector, rather than preceding it."
    ],
    "qualified_inference": [
      "Windows subsequently sent AoA b75f and no replacement for channel a0dc was observed. This is consistent with false prefix feedback suppressing repair; Windows internal retry/receipt state was not traced."
    ],
    "limits": [
      "The full ledger is strictly invalid due to the first ARM/RX timestamp reversal. Only the later monotonic excerpt is used, without changing the original.",
      "Capture absence is limited to recorded interfaces/intervals; pre/post-netem positioning depends on the recorded owned network setup.",
      "Encrypted bodies are snap-truncated. No decrypted application integrity claim is made.",
      "Windows flag0x200 is retained as opaque; correlated visible layouts have no extra field for it.",
      "Packet identity and byte-exact vector matching establish chronology without assuming a sub-millisecond pcap/ledger clock alignment."
    ]
  }
